/// The Unicode tables the scanner needs, and the operations over them.
///
/// ALGOL-24.md, Identifiers: a letter is a Unicode code point in category Lu,
/// Ll, Lt, Lm or Lo, plus `_`, `?` and `!`; a digit is ASCII `0`-`9` and a
/// Unicode digit is not an identifier character at all. The case fold covers
/// the whole letter range and is locale-independent -- `I` never folds to `i`
/// with no dot. Both tables below are generated from Unicode 15.0.0.
///
/// ⚠️ GENERATED. Do not hand-edit a row; regenerate both tables together. The
/// recipe is Python's own database, so it needs no checked-in data file:
///
///     import unicodedata
///     L = {'Lu','Ll','Lt','Lm','Lo'}
///     letters = [c for c in range(0x110000)
///                if unicodedata.category(chr(c)) in L]
///     folds   = [(chr(c), chr(c).casefold()) for c in range(0x80, 0x110000)
///                if unicodedata.category(chr(c)) in L
///                and chr(c).casefold() != chr(c)]
///
/// LETTER_RANGES is those code points collapsed into 659 inclusive
/// [low, high] pairs, flattened, ascending, and searched by bisection.
/// CASE_FOLDS is 1461 entries keyed by the character's UTF-8 bytes.
///
/// ⚠️ FULL case folding, not simple, and the difference is visible: `ß` folds
/// to `ss`, so `Straße` and `STRASSE` are one name. ALGOL-24.md names `Straße`
/// and `İstanbul` as the cases the fold must cover, and under SIMPLE folding
/// those are the two characters the fold does nothing to -- a strange pair to
/// have chosen -- so full is the reading taken. Recorded because it is
/// invisible until someone writes German. See issue #2.
///
/// ⚠️ Folding is not normalization, and the language performs none. `İ` folds
/// to `i` followed by a combining dot, which is NOT the same name as `istanbul`.
/// ALGOL-24.md, Identifiers: "two names that render identically but differ in
/// normalization are different names".
///
/// ⚠️ ASCII never reaches CASE_FOLDS. FoldOf lower-cases it arithmetically, so
/// the table starts at U+0080 and the common identifier costs no Map lookup at
/// all. Adding ASCII rows here would be dead weight on the scanner's hot path.
///
